There exists at least one version of the wizard patch in which it did
indeed work that way; there was a prompt for skilled casters "You feel
you could cast this spell with extra oomph. Try?". A response of 'y'
led to the throwspell() behaviour, 'n' fell through to the zap-effect.

I don't know the exact chronology involved in the development of the
wizard patch, however, or which of Josh Fishman's, Larry
Stewart-Zerba's, or Warwick Allison's work should be considered the
"official" version of it; but compare http://tinyurl.com/3rsn9
(Fishman 0.71) with http://www.juiblex.co.uk/nethack/Wizard/wiz10.txt
(Stewart-Zerba/Allison 1.0).

> Unfortunately, all this doesn't directly address the differences between RAY 
> and DISTANT types of spellcasting. (The comment seems to address all spell 
> effects, although it does come immediately before cone of cold and fireball 
> code).

As far as the current implementation goes, only those two spells have
a skilled effect that is not a strict superset of the unskilled effect.

(Remove curse is the only case where I wouldn't consider that superset
to be necessarily strictly better, if you have something in your
inventory which you'd prefer to keep cursed; but that's easily enough
worked around.)

> The interface issue is why I suggested that targetting a square 
> immediately adjacent to the hero would be equivalent to a directional 
> command and invoke the RAY effect: no need for a special prompt. Why else 
> would you target such a square? If you want to cast the spell on yourself, 
> you could still choose "." as your direction; if a monster is immediately 
> adjacent to you, the ray would still terminate on that square and explode.

You might want to get the multiple-fireball effect on a monster (or
unoccupied square, if that helps target multiple monsters) adjacent to
you. If, say, the situation is:

.....
.MMM.
.@*..
.MMM.
.....

and you're fire-resistant with nothing flammable in inventory,
targetting skilled fireball at the square with the gem on it is the
way to go.

